31. Recommended Large-Result Architecture

Instead of:

get_holdings()

returning everything, use:

get_holdings(
    account_id=None,
    symbols=None,
    fields=None,
    limit=100,
    cursor=None,
)

The tool can support:

Filtering
Field projection
Pagination
Sorting
Search
Cursor-based continuation

⸻

32. Example Paginated Result

Instead of:

{
  "holdings": [
    "... thousands of records ..."
  ]
}

return:

{
  "items": [
    {
      "symbol": "AAPL",
      "quantity": 100,
      "market_value": 22000
    },
    {
      "symbol": "MSFT",
      "quantity": 50,
      "market_value": 21000
    }
  ],
  "has_more": true,
  "next_cursor": "eyJwYWdlIjoyfQ=="
}

Claude can then request another page only if necessary.

⸻

33. Cursor-Based Pagination

Cursor pagination is generally preferable for large changing datasets.

Example:

Claude
get_holdings(
    limit=100
)
        |
        v
MCP Server
items = first 100
next_cursor = "abc123"
        |
        v
Claude determines more data is needed
        |
        v
get_holdings(
    limit=100,
    cursor="abc123"
)
        |
        v
next 100 records

This keeps individual tool results manageable.

⸻

34. Filtering

Allow Claude to reduce the dataset before returning it.

Example:

get_holdings(
    asset_type="equity",
    limit=100
)

instead of:

Retrieve 20,000 records
        |
        v
Send everything to Claude
        |
        v
Ask Claude to filter equities

Prefer:

Claude specifies filter
        |
        v
MCP Server
        |
        v
Upstream filtering
        |
        v
Only relevant records
        |
        v
Claude

