35. Field Projection

If an API contains 40 fields but Claude needs only five, do not necessarily return all 40.

For example:

get_holdings(
    fields=[
        "symbol",
        "quantity",
        "market_value",
        "asset_type"
    ]
)

This can significantly reduce MCP output size.

⸻

36. Server-Side Aggregation

For analytical questions, returning raw records may not always be necessary.

Suppose the user asks:

What are my five largest holdings?

Instead of:

20,000 holdings
        |
        v
Claude
        |
        v
Sort 20,000 records

consider:

User request
      |
      v
MCP tool
      |
      v
Upstream query / server-side sorting
      |
      v
Top 5 holdings
      |
      v
Claude

For example:

get_holdings(
    sort_by="market_value",
    sort_order="desc",
    limit=5
)

This reduces:

* token usage
* latency
* context consumption
* truncation risk
* unnecessary data exposure

⸻

37. On-Demand Detail Retrieval

Another useful pattern is:

Search / list tool
       |
       v
Small summary objects
       |
       v
Claude identifies relevant records
       |
       v
Detail tool
       |
       v
Full information for selected records

For example:

search_accounts()

returns:

[
  {
    "account_id": "123",
    "name": "Brokerage"
  },
  {
    "account_id": "456",
    "name": "Retirement"
  }
]

Then:

get_account_details(account_id="123")

returns only the necessary details.

However, do not introduce unnecessary multi-tool orchestration merely to reduce response size.

If a single tool can naturally support optional filters, that may provide a better interface.

